WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.752
Retrieval can only find what ingestion kept.

2
00:00:03.032 --> 00:00:07.107
Week 1 gave you embeddings and similarity search over clean strings.

3
00:00:07.387 --> 00:00:19.461
Real knowledge lives in PDFs exported from slide decks, scanned contracts, wiki pages wrapped in navigation menus, spreadsheets, and the same policy saved in four places.

4
00:00:19.741 --> 00:00:30.259
Ingestion is everything between “a file exists” and “clean text with metadata is ready to chunk”: parsing, cleaning, attaching metadata, and removing duplicates.

5
00:00:30.539 --> 00:00:34.635
It deserves a whole lesson because it sets a ceiling on everything after it.

6
00:00:34.915 --> 00:00:39.779
If the parser dropped a table, no embedding model will retrieve the number that was in it.

7
00:00:40.059 --> 00:00:46.331
If a scanned page came back empty, the document silently does not exist as far as search is concerned.

8
00:00:46.611 --> 00:00:55.315
If a navigation menu was kept, every chunk from that site shares the same twenty words of noise, and similarity scores drift toward boilerplate.

9
00:00:55.595 --> 00:01:02.037
Teams that debug retrieval quality by swapping embedding models often find the problem was upstream.

10
00:01:02.317 --> 00:01:05.752
Before building any of this, ask whether you need retrieval at all.

11
00:01:06.032 --> 00:01:25.296
Anthropic’s Contextual Retrieval post makes a point worth repeating: if the whole knowledge base fits in the prompt (it suggests a rough threshold of 200,000 tokens, about 500 pages), you can often skip retrieval and send everything, using prompt caching to keep it affordable.

12
00:01:25.576 --> 00:01:32.979
Ingestion still matters in that case, since the model reads whatever your parser produced, but chunking and vector stores may not.

13
00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:42.517
This lesson works through the formats in order of difficulty, then covers the three things you add on the way out: cleaning checks, metadata, and deduplication.

14
00:01:42.797 --> 00:01:47.320
The lab at the end of the week builds the whole pipeline on a small fictional corpus.
